WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.903
Secure firmware starts with a clear model
of assets and trust boundaries.

2
00:00:03.983 --> 00:00:07.167
A device may protect keys, configuration,
measurements, user data,

3
00:00:07.247 --> 00:00:10.540
safety-relevant outputs, network identity,
intellectual property,

4
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:14.572
service availability, or the integrity of
actions it performs in the physical world.

5
00:00:14.652 --> 00:00:17.287
Boundaries appear across the entire
product lifecycle.

6
00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:20.825
Boot, runtime commands, update handling,
provisioning, manufacturing,

7
00:00:20.905 --> 00:00:24.857
diagnostics, debug, cloud communication,
local service tools, field maintenance,

8
00:00:24.937 --> 00:00:27.682
and end-of-life handling can each
introduce assumptions.

9
00:00:27.762 --> 00:00:31.549
If those assumptions stay informal, they
are difficult to test and easy to forget.

10
00:00:31.629 --> 00:00:35.307
Physical access assumptions should be
stated rather than silently inherited.

11
00:00:35.387 --> 00:00:37.637
Some devices operate in controlled
facilities.

12
00:00:37.717 --> 00:00:41.230
Others are installed in public, customer,
remote, or mobile environments.

13
00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:44.329
The expected access model influences debug
policy, key storage,

14
00:00:44.409 --> 00:00:48.197
diagnostic behavior, update recovery, and
what data should remain on the device.

15
00:00:48.277 --> 00:00:50.747
Interfaces should be reviewed as part of
the same map.

16
00:00:50.827 --> 00:00:53.626
Local buttons, wired buses, wireless
links, service ports,

17
00:00:53.706 --> 00:00:56.890
maintenance commands, update channels,
cloud APIs, storage media,

18
00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.604
and manufacturing tools may all affect
device behavior.

19
00:00:59.684 --> 00:01:03.636
Even interfaces that are not
internet-facing can become important if they influence

20
00:01:03.716 --> 00:01:05.747
configuration, identity, or update state.

21
00:01:05.827 --> 00:01:08.791
A useful firmware threat model does not
need dramatic language.

22
00:01:08.871 --> 00:01:12.659
It needs to turn lifecycle assumptions
into engineering requirements that can be

23
00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:15.044
implemented, reviewed, tested, and
maintained.

24
00:01:15.124 --> 00:01:18.143
The result should help teams decide what
to validate, what to log,

25
00:01:18.223 --> 00:01:19.650
what to bound, what to protect,

26
00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.420
and what evidence to keep for support and
security review.
